 * IEEE 802.11 support (Linux-specific code)
 *//* XXX for ARPHRD_* *//*
 * Print a console message with the device name prepended.
 *//* XXX *//*
 * Allocate a data frame
 * Returns the sk_buff and a pointer to the start of the reserved contiguous data area.
 *//*
 * Allocate a management frame
 * Returns the sk_buff and a pointer to the start of the reserved contiguous data area.
 * The data area is forced to 32-bit alignment and the buffer length to a multiple of 4 bytes.  This
 * is done mainly so beacon frames (that require this) can use this interface too.
 *//* Cache align the frame *//*
 * VLAN support.
 *//*
 * Add an rx vlan identifier
 *//*
 * Kill (i.e. delete) a vlan identifier.
 *//* IEEE80211_VLAN_TAG_USED *//* IEEE80211_VLAN_TAG_USED *//* fire off wireless event station leaving *//* dispatch wireless event indicating scan completed *//* TODO: needed parameters: count, keyid, key type, src address, TSC *//**
 * Entry point for reporting of MIC failure (via ic pointer).
 *//* If more than two errors, only report the first two as this
	 * will be enough to trigger countermeasures.
	 *//* Send up to 2 reports *//* Format a frame header appropriately for a MIC report *//* TODO: needed parameters: count, keyid, key type, src address, TSC *//* qtn=1 is used (in conjunction with userspace hostapd change) to ensure that the
	 * MIC failure report is tallied, regardless of the source address.
	 *//*
 * Note that a successful call to this function does not guarantee that
 * the services provided by the requested module are available:
 *
 * "Note that a successful module load does not mean the module did not
 * then unload and exit on an error of its own. Callers must check that
 * the service they requested is now available not blindly invoke it."
 * http://kernelnewbies.org/documents/kdoc/kernel-api/r7338.html
 *//* CONFIG_KMOD *//* CONFIG_KMOD *//**
 * Return a string representing the MIMO power save mode passed in.
 *//**
 * Return a STATIC buffer containing a dump of the HT capability info field
 *//* FIXME: decode other parts of the capability IE here *//* Check whether to output a node entry for the /proc/net/madwifi/wifi0/associated_sta output *//* Don't print anything out on the STA side if we're not connected. *///IEEE80211_NODE_LOCK(nt);/* Assume each node needs 500 bytes *//* Nodes associated with OUR VAP, not with OUR MAC address, and with
		 * NON-ZERO association ID (ie no temporary nodes)
		 *///IEEE80211_NODE_UNLOCK(nt);/* initially allocate both read and write buffers *//* now read the data into the buffer *//* IEEE80211_DEBUG *//* cannot be CTL_ANY or CTL_NONE *//* NB: must be last entry before NULL *//*
	 * Reserve space for the device name outside the net_device structure
	 * so that if the name changes we know what it used to be.
	 *//* setup the table *//* [1] is NULL terminator *//* XXX bad idea? *//* [3] is NULL terminator *//* copy in pre-defined data *//* add in dynamic data references *//* XXX? *//* and register everything *//* Ensure the base madwifi directory exists *//* Create a proc directory named after the VAP *//* Create a proc entry listing the associated stations *//* Recreate any other proc entries that have been registered *//* Frees all memory used for the list of proc entries *//* Called by other modules to register a proc entry under the vap directory *//* Ignore if already in the list *//* Check for end of list *//* Otherwise move on *//* Create an item in our list for the new entry *//* Create the entry record *//* Create the actual proc entry *//* Add it to the list *//* Add to the start *//* Add to the end *//* Remove child proc entries but leave them in the list *//*
 * Format an Ethernet MAC for printing.
 *//* XXX *//* XXX *//* Function to handle the device event notifications.
 * If the event is a NETDEV_CHANGENAME, and is for an interface
 * we are taking care of, then we want to remove its existing
 * proc entries (which now have the wrong names) and add
 * new, correct, entries.
 *//*
 * Module glue.
 *//* The legacy notifier block emits an ARC module-section relocation that
	 * the 5.10 loader leaves as a low section-relative pointer.  No VAP exists
	 * at module load, so defer notifier registration until the native 5.10
	 * device lifecycle port is installed. *//*-
