 * Node reference counting definitions.
 *
 * ieee80211_node_initref	initialize the reference count to 1
 * ieee80211_node_incref	add a reference
 * ieee80211_node_dectestref	remove a reference and return 1 if this
 *				is the last reference, otherwise 0
 * ieee80211_node_refcnt	reference count for printing (only)
 *//*
 * Linux has no equivalents to malloc types so null these out.
 *//*
 * flags to malloc.
 *//* do not block *//* ok to block *//* bzero the allocation *//* Debug memory alloc/free. Keeps a running total of allocated/freed
 * dynamic memory on the WLAN driver.
 *//* Total number of bytes allocated using the MALLOC macro *//* Total number of bytes freed using the FREE macro *//* g_wlan_tot_alloc - g_wlan_tot_free - convenience value *//* WLAN driver malloc - chain into kmalloc while supporting extra
 * features such as zeroing the memory. 
 *//* Debug version of the MALLOC - add in extra fields for accounting *//* The memory looks like: | Size | Magic | Block ..... | 
	 * Where magic is 0xDEADBEEF and is used to ensure we're only
	 * doing accounting/pointer juggling on blocks allocated using
	 * this MALLOC
	 *//* First extra word is the length of the allocation *//* Magic value for making free work properly *//* Accounting *//* This is NOT thread safe *//* Only do pointer juggling for blocks allocated using MALLOC *//* This is NOT thread safe *//* Invalid - block not allocated using 'MALLOC' passed in *///printk("Free %p\n", p_size);/*
 * This unlikely to be popular but it dramatically reduces diffs.
 *//*
 * Queue write-arounds and support routines.
 *//*
 * List of flags we want to share for kernel threads,
 * if only because they are not used by them anyway.
