 *//*
 * Prototypes and definitions for BSD/OS-compatible network interface
 * media selection.
 *
 * Where it is safe to do so, this code strays slightly from the BSD/OS
 * design.  Software which uses the API (device drivers, basically)
 * shouldn't notice any difference.
 *
 * Many thanks to Matt Thomas for providing the information necessary
 * to implement this interface.
 *//* if name, e.g. "en0" *//* current media options *//* don't care mask *//* media status *//* active options *//* # entries in ifm_ulist array *//* media words *//* set net media *//* get net media *//*
 * Driver callbacks for media status and change requests.
 *//*
 * In-kernel representation of a single supported media type.
 *//* description of this media attachment *//* for driver-specific use *//* for driver-specific use *//*
 * One of these goes into a network interface's softc structure.
 * It is used to keep general media state.
 *//* mask of changes we don't care about *//* current user-set media word *//* currently selected media *//* list of all supported media *//* media change driver callback *//* media status driver callback *//* Initialize an interface's struct if_media field. *//* Remove all mediums from a struct ifmedia.  *//* Add one supported medium to a struct ifmedia. *//* Add an array (of ifmedia_entry) media to a struct ifmedia. *//* Set default media type on initialization. *//* Common ioctl function for getting/setting media, called by driver. *//*_KERNEL *//*
 * if_media Options word:
 *	Bits	Use
 *	----	-------
 *	0-4	Media variant
 *	5-7	Media type
 *	8-15	Type specific options
 *	16-18	Mode (for multi-mode devices)
 *	19	RFU
 *	20-27	Shared (global) options
 *	28-31	Instance
 *//*
 * Ethernet
 *//* 10BaseT - RJ45 *//* 10Base2 - Thinnet *//* 10Base5 - AUI *//* 100BaseTX - RJ45 *//* 100BaseFX - Fiber *//* 100BaseT4 - 4 pair cat 3 *//* 100VG-AnyLAN *//* 100BaseT2 *//* 1000BaseSX - multi-mode fiber *//* 10BaseT over shielded TP *//* 10BaseFL - Fiber *//* 1000baseLX - single-mode fiber *//* 1000baseCX - 150ohm STP *//* 1000baseT - 4 pair cat 5 *//* HomePNA 1.0 (1Mb/s) *//* note 31 is the max! *//* master mode (1000baseT) *//*
 * Token ring
 *//* Shielded twisted pair 4m - DB9 *//* Shielded twisted pair 16m - DB9 *//* Unshielded twisted pair 4m - RJ45 *//* Unshielded twisted pair 16m - RJ45 *//* Shielded twisted pair 100m - DB9 *//* Unshielded twisted pair 100m - RJ45 *//* Early token release *//* Enable source routing features *//* All routes / Single route bcast *//* Dedicated token ring *//* Classic token ring *//* Automatic Dedicate/Classic token ring *//*
 * FDDI
 *//* Single-mode fiber *//* Multi-mode fiber *//* CDDI / UTP *//* Dual attach / single attach *//*
 * IEEE 802.11 Wireless
 *//* NB: 0,1,2 are auto, manual, none defined below *//* Frequency Hopping 1Mbps *//* Frequency Hopping 2Mbps *//* Direct Sequence 1Mbps *//* Direct Sequence 2Mbps *//* Direct Sequence 5.5Mbps *//* Direct Sequence 11Mbps *//* Direct Sequence 22Mbps *//* OFDM 1.5Mbps *//* OFDM 2.25Mbps *//* OFDM 3Mbps *//* OFDM 4.5Mbps *//* OFDM 6Mbps *//* OFDM 9Mbps *//* OFDM 12Mbps *//* OFDM 13.5Mpbs *//* OFDM 18Mbps *//* OFDM 24Mbps *//* OFDM 27Mbps *//* OFDM 36Mbps *//* OFDM 48Mbps *//* OFDM 54Mbps *//* OFDM 72Mbps *//* 11N - MCS 0  -6.5Mbps *//* 11N - MCS 1  -13Mbps *//* 11N - MCS 2  -19.5Mbps *//* 11N - MCS 3  -26Mbps *//* 11N - MCS 4  -39Mbps *//* 11N - MCS 5  -52Mbps *//* 11N - MCS 6  -58.5Mbps *//* 11N - MCS 7  -65Mbps *//* 11N - MCS 8  -13Mbps *//* 11N - MCS 9  -26Mbps *//* 11N - MCS 10 -39Mbps *//* 11N - MCS 11 -52Mbps *//* 11N - MCS 12 -78Mbps *//* 11N - MCS 13 -104Mbps *//* 11N - MCS 14 -117Mbps *//* 11N - MCS 15 -130Mbps *//* Operate in Adhoc mode *//* Operate in Host AP mode *//* Operate in IBSS mode *//* Operate in WDS mode *//* Operate in turbo mode *//* Operate in monitor mode *//* operating mode for multi-mode devices *//* 5Ghz, OFDM mode *//* Direct Sequence mode *//* 2Ghz, CCK mode *//* 2Ghz, GFSK mode *//* 5Ghz, OFDM mode, HT *//* 2Ghz, OFDM mode, HT *//* 2G HT40 *//* 5G HT40 *//* 5G VHT20 *//* 5G VHT40 *//* 5G VHT80 *//* 5G VHT160 *//*
 * Shared media sub-types
 *//* Autoselect best media *//* Jumper/dipswitch selects media *//* Deselect all media *//*
 * Shared options
 *//* Force full duplex *//* Force half duplex *//* Driver defined flag *//* Driver defined flag *//* Driver defined flag *//* Put hardware in loopback *//*
 * Masks
 *//* Network type *//* Media sub-type *//* Instance *//* Instance shift *//* Type specific options *//* Mode *//* Mode shift *//* Global options *//*
 * Status bits
 *//* Active bit valid *//* Interface attached to working net *//*
 * Macros to extract various bits of information from the media word.
 *//*
 * Macro to create a media word.
 *//*
 * NetBSD extension not defined in the BSDI API.  This is used in various
 * places to get the canonical description for a given type/subtype.
 *
 * NOTE: all but the top-level type descriptions must contain NO whitespace!
 * Otherwise, parsing these in ifconfig(8) would be a nightmare.
 *//* word value; may be masked *//* description *//* _NET_IF_MEDIA_H_ *//*
