 *//* Driver name *//* Swap bytes *//* Timeout *//* Each flash chip has same page size *//* Set zero if want to disable log messages *//* Structure which holds all allocated for SPI flash resources.
*
* Access to SPI controller must be only when hold 'lock' mutex.
*
* To read data cached ioremapped 'mem_read_cache' is used.
* Why cached - because ARC processor works more efficient
* with AMBA bus when access comes through d-cache.
*
* To write data NON-cached ioremapped 'mem_write_nocache' is used.
* Why non-cached - because in Linux process (if not be extra-cautious)
* can be interrupted or switched very easily when half cache-line is
* updated only. This half-updated cache-line can be flushed (because
* it is dirty). Flush means 'write'. So half-updated data will be stored
* on flash. It is not a problem in case of reading (see cached 'mem_read_cache')
* as after cache-line invalidating FAST_READ SPI command will be issued again
* and we will have only slight performance penalty. And it is real problem
* when do writing. So for writing let's have non-cached memory area.
* Disadvantage - we use twice more virtual memory (should not be problem on Ruby).
*//* ARC Cache uses physical addresses *//* Pre-condition. *//* Request is out of range. *//* Although it is legal to have erase address
		* inside sector, it is not very safe.
		* Simple mistake - and neighbour sector erased.
		*//* Begin synchronized block *//* Going to bypass d-cache, so invalidate it before. *//* Erasing *//* Bulk erase *//* Per-sector erase *//* End synchronized block *//* Pre-condition. *//* Request is out of range. *//* Calculate read length *//* Begin synchronized block *//* Reading */// #ifdef CONFIG_PREEMPT/* End synchronized block *//* Pre-condition. *//* Request is out of range. *//* Calculate write length *//* Begin synchronized block *//* Going to bypass d-cache, so invalidate it before. *//* Writing *//* Per-page programming *//* do not exceed page boundary *//* do not exceed requested range *//*
		 * memcpy((u_char *)flash->mem_write_nocache + write_begin, buf, iter_write_len);
		 * memcpy doesn't work correctly here for Linux_2.6.35.12, due to implementation of it.
		 *//* End synchronized block *//* Begin synchronized block *//* Make sure that all pending write/erase transactions are finished *//* End synchronized block *//* Allocate structure to hold flash specific data. *//* Cannot setup proper clock yet, so set up slowest possible mode. *//* Get flash information. *//* Now we are ready to setup correct frequency. *//* Map flash memory. We need both cached and non-cached access. *//* Initialize mutex */// prioritise paritions being sent as command line parameters// provide a default table if command line parameter fails/* If flag is set, enable support Protection mode *//* No Protection *//* We can have only single flash chip connected to SPI controller *//******************************************************************************
* Function:spi_api_flash_status
* Purpose: read status of te Flash
* Returns: TIMEOUT or success
* Note:
* ***********************************************************************************//* For external use to query flash size *//*
 * (C) Copyright 2013 Quantenna Communications Inc.
