 *//*
 * 802.11 protocol crypto-related definitions.
 *//* space for both tx+rx keys *//* total number of TIDs *//*
 * Old WEP-style key.  Deprecated.
 *//* key length in bytes *//*
 * Crypto key state.  There is sufficient room for all supported
 * ciphers (see below).  The underlying ciphers are handled
 * separately through loadable cipher modules that register with
 * the generic crypto support.  A key has a reference to an instance
 * of the cipher; any per-key state is hung off wk_private by the
 * cipher when it is attached.  Ciphers are automatically called
 * to detach and cleanup any such state when the key is deleted.
 *
 * The generic crypto support handles encap/decap of cipher-related
 * frame contents for both hardware- and software-based implementations.
 * A key requiring software crypto support is automatically flagged and
 * the cipher is expected to honor this and do the necessary work.
 * Ciphers such as TKIP may also support mixed hardware/software
 * encrypt/decrypt and MIC processing.
 *
 * Note: This definition must be the same as qtn_key.
 *//* key length in bytes *//* key used for xmit *//* key used for recv *//* key used for WPA group operation *//* host-based encrypt/decrypt *//* host-based enmic/demic *//* VLAN group key *//* key index *//* key receive sequence counter *//* key transmit sequence counter *//* private cipher state *//* common flags passed in by apps *//*
 * NB: these values are ordered carefully; there are lots of
 * of implications in any reordering.  In particular beware
 * that 4 is not used to avoid conflicting with IEEE80211_F_PRIVACY.
 *//* pseudo value *//*
 * Template for a supported cipher.  Ciphers register with the
 * crypto code and are typically loaded as separate modules
 * (the null cipher is always present).
 * XXX may need refcnts
 *//* printable name *//* IEEE80211_CIPHER_* *//* size of privacy header (bytes) *//* size of privacy trailer (bytes) *//* size of mic trailer (bytes) *//*
 * Check and remove any MIC.
 *//*
 * Add any MIC.
 *//* 
 * Reset key state to an unused state.  The crypto
 * key allocation mechanism ensures other state (e.g.
 * key data) is properly setup before a key is used.
 *//*
 * Crypto-related notification methods.
